            <ArticleTitle>I are we closer to developing threshold limit values for allergens in the workplace?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>11-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To define threshold limit values and legally binding occupational exposure limits.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA SOURCES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Review of suitable literature.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY SELECTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Studies based on detailed descriptions and/or measurements of airborne allergenic dust, total allergens, or even key allergens were selected.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Prevalences of IgE-mediated sensitization and occupational asthma are related to the aeroallergen load in workplaces. Data to set threshold limit values for flour, latex, a-amylase, and isocyanates are already sufficiently available.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">To optimize primary prevention in workplaces,health-based occupational exposure limits should be set for major occupational allergens.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
